surface: Surface
	description=What the outside world touches. One ASGI callable, the routes behind it, and the HTML that goes back.
	asgi-app: ASGI app
		description=The single callable uvicorn calls. It configures logging once, builds the core app once, then delegates.
		example=`vyasa/main.py` defines `async def app(scope, receive, send)`.
	content-route: Content route
		description=The handler for one document URL. It resolves the path, checks access, reads the file, and renders it.
		example=`render_post_detail` in `vyasa/content_routes.py` handles `/posts/<slug>`.
	page-frame: Page frame
		description=The rendered body plus the chrome it asked for, before the shell wraps it.
		example=`PageFrame` and `PageFrameDeps` in `vyasa/page_frame.py`.
	page-shell: Page shell
		description=The shared outer document: head, navbar, sidebar, footer. Core owns it so every page agrees.
		example=`PageShellModel` and `StaticShellRenderer` in `vyasa/page_shell.py`.
	html-response: HTML response
		description=The finished page the browser receives.
		example=A full document, or an htmx fragment when the request came from a link inside the site.
core: Core
	description=The mandatory runtime layer. It owns routing, the page shell, content mounting, and access control. Core must not depend on any one extension.
	core-app: Core app
		description=The FastHTML application core builds at start, before extensions register anything.
		example=`_build_app` and `_initialize_app` in `vyasa/core.py`.
	beforeware: Beforeware
		description=The per-request hook that runs before any handler.
		example=`_build_beforeware` in `vyasa/core.py`.
	rbac: RBAC
		description=Role rules that decide whether this person may see this path. Core-owned, so folder configuration cannot switch it off.
		example=`is_allowed` against `rbac_rules`; the store lives in `vyasa/rbac_store.py`.
	content-mounts: Content mounts
		description=The map from a URL slug to a real directory on disk. More than one root can be mounted.
		example=`get_content_mounts` and `content_root_and_relative` in `vyasa/helpers.py`.
	asset-collector: Asset collector
		description=The per-render object where anything that renders records which asset bundle the page needs. Nothing loads globally.
		example=`AssetCollector` in `vyasa/extensions.py`.
	reload-hub: Reload hub
		description=The watcher and fan-out that tells open pages a file changed.
		example=`_ReloadHub` and `_live_reload_payload` in `vyasa/core.py`.
pipeline: Markdown pipeline
	description=The core-owned contract that turns Markdown into HTML. Extensions add syntax to it; they do not replace it.
	frontmatter: Frontmatter
		description=The metadata block at the top of a document. It sets the title and the per-page switches.
		example=`parse_frontmatter`, called by `render_post_detail` before rendering.
	from-md: from_md
		description=The one entry point that renders Markdown text to HTML nodes.
		example=`from_md` in `vyasa/extensions_builtin/markdown/renderer.py`.
	fence-handler: Fence handler
		description=The dispatcher for a fenced block. One syntax marker has exactly one owning extension.
		example=`split_fence_frontmatter` in `vyasa/markdown_fence.py` splits a fence before its owner reads it.
	wikilink-resolver: Wikilink resolver
		description=Turns a double-bracket link into a real URL against the content mounts.
		note=CONTEXT.md lists wikilinks as core-owned, but the code lives in `extensions_builtin/wikilinks`. The document and the tree disagree.
		example=`[[some-page]]` becomes `/posts/some-page`.
	asset-url: Asset URL
		description=The core-managed URL an extension asset is served from. Extensions never invent their own static route.
		example=`extension_asset_url` in `vyasa/assets.py`.
extension: Extensions
	description=Optional modules that add behavior through core contracts. Each is switched on explicitly and can be switched off.
	extension-runtime: Extension runtime
		description=Resolves which extensions are enabled, validates their metadata, and orders them. Runs once at start.
		example=`ExtensionRuntime` and `refresh_extension_runtime` in `vyasa/extensions.py`.
	extension-app: Extension app
		description=The restricted surface an extension registers through. It never sees the raw FastHTML app.
		example=`VyasaExtensionBase` plus the registrar classes in `vyasa/extensions.py`.
	route-registrar: Route registrar
		description=Takes route registrations and declares a prefix, so start-up can catch two extensions claiming one path.
		example=`_RouteRegistrar` in `vyasa/extensions.py`.
	markdown-registrar: Markdown registrar
		description=Claims a fence marker for one extension.
		example=`_MarkdownRegistrar` in `vyasa/extensions.py`.
	asset-registrar: Asset registrar
		description=Declares the CSS and JS bundles an extension can serve, and the directory they come from.
		example=`_AssetRegistrar` in `vyasa/extensions.py`.
	tasks-extension: Tasks extension
		description=Renders a knowledge-graph pack as an interactive canvas. The largest built-in.
		example=`vyasa/extensions_builtin/tasks`; `tasks.js` alone is over nine thousand lines.
	search-extension: Search extension
		description=Indexes the mounted content and answers queries. If it breaks, core disables search rather than failing the site.
		example=`vyasa/extensions_builtin/default_search`.
	layout-extension: Layout extension
		description=Decides page structure and navigation surfaces. At most one is active, and boot fails without one.
		example=`vyasa/extensions_builtin/default_layout.py`.
content: Content
	description=Where documents actually live. Core reads through a backend protocol, so a working tree and a git ref look the same.
	content-backend: Content backend
		description=The protocol every source of documents satisfies.
		example=`ContentBackend` and `backend_for` in `vyasa/content_backend.py`.
	filesystem-backend: Filesystem backend
		description=Reads the working tree directly.
		example=`FilesystemBackend` in `vyasa/content_backend.py`.
	git-backend: Git backend
		description=Reads a document out of a git ref instead of the working tree.
		example=`GitBackend`, reached through `discover_git_backend`.
	virtual-path: Virtual path
		description=A path-shaped object over git object storage, so the rest of the code needs no special case.
		note=This is the seam that lets one render path serve both a live file and a committed one.
		example=`VirtualPath` and `_VStat` in `vyasa/content_backend.py`.
	markdown-file: Markdown file
		description=One document on disk.
		example=`demo/vyasa-architecture.md`.
	kg-pack: Knowledge graph pack
		description=A folder of nodes, edges, attributes, schema, and palette that a fence renders as a graph.
		example=This pack, `demo/vyasa-architecture.kg`.
configuration: Configuration
	description=The declarations that decide what loads and how it behaves. It crosses every layer, which is why it is drawn beside the stack and not inside it.
	root-config: Root .vyasa
		description=The root file that decides which extensions load at server start. Nothing is discovered implicitly.
		example=`[extensions.tasks] enabled = true`.
	folder-config: Folder .vyasa
		description=A folder-local file that tunes behavior already loaded, for one subtree. It cannot disable core RBAC.
		example=A docs folder turning off the sidebar without touching the rest of the site.
	extension-meta: Extension metadata
		description=Python-declared capabilities, slots, requirements, and fallbacks. The rules live in code, not in TOML.
		example=`ExtensionMeta` in `vyasa/extensions.py`.
	capability-name: Capability name
		description=A stable string naming a slot, capability, or bundle. Extensions depend on these, not on extension ids.
		example=`slot:layout`, `cap:search`, `bundle:tasks`.
	storage-namespace: Storage namespace
		description=A durable directory core assigns to one extension, so local data cannot collide.
		example=`StorageNamespace` in `vyasa/extensions.py`.
